While and for loops are both used to iterate over a sequence of elements, but they serve different purposes and have different syntax.

A while loop executes as long as a certain condition is true, whereas a for loop iterates over a sequence of elements, such as an array or a string.

In a while loop, the condition is evaluated at the beginning of each iteration, and if it's true, the code within the loop body is executed. The loop continues until the condition becomes false.

In contrast, a for loop iterates over a sequence and executes the code within the loop body for each element in the sequence. The loop body is executed once for each element in the sequence.

For example, consider an array of numbers from 1 to 5:

```
var numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

// While loop
var i = 0;
while (i < numbers.length) {
  console.log(numbers[i]);
  i++;
}

// For loop
for (var i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
  console.log(numbers[i]);
}
```

In this example, both loops will output the numbers 1 to 5, but the while loop uses a variable `i` to keep track of the current index, whereas the for loop uses the built-in `length` property of the array to determine when to stop iterating.

Here is a Java program that prints all permutations of an array:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] array = {1, 2, 3};
        permute(array, 0, array.length - 1);
    }

    public static void permute(int[] array, int left, int right) {
        if (left == right)
            System.out.println(Arrays.toString(array));
        else {
            for (int i = left; i <= right; i++) {
                swap(array, left, i);
                permute(array, left + 1, right);
                swap(array, left, i);
            }
        }
    }

    public static void swap(int[] array, int i, int j) {
        int temp = array[i];
        array[i] = array[j];
        array[j] = temp;
    }
}
```

This program uses a recursive approach to generate all permutations of the array. It uses the swap function to swap the elements at the current index and the remaining unprocessed elements. The base case for the recursion is when the left and right indices meet, at which point the permutation is printed.

